  {
   "id": "rIxrjw6xCAAJ",
   "from": "Len Thomas",
   "author": "7482eee8d2863d12",
   "date": "2018-07-04T17:09:45Z",
   "subject": "Re: [distance-sampling] Line transects visited several times",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "This question comes up quite a bit.  Just pretend to Distance you did 21 \r<br>line transects, but double the line length you enter in the data sheet. \r<br>Cheers, Len\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "\r<br>On 04/07/2018 17:58, Gilles Saulas wrote:\r<br>&gt; Hello\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; In a recent study ), we did 21 line transects twice. For each, how to \r<br>&gt; indicate that it is 2 times the same line transect and not 2 different \r<br>&gt; line transects ? I try to use the parameter “multiplier” but the \r<br>&gt; analysis gives the same result (Distance v 7.1).\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; In fact, some line transects were done twice, others only once; so, how \r<br>&gt; to do ?\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; Thank you in advance for your help.\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; Best regards,\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; Gilles Saulas\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "-- \r<br>Len Thomas  <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">len.t...@st-andrews.ac.uk</a>  <a href=\"http://lenthomas.org\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://lenthomas.org&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792474441153000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3iqYz7FfbwF-aHSYBvPNoP\">lenthomas.org</a>  @len_thom\r<br>Centre for Research into Ecological and Environmental Modelling\r<br>The Observatory, University of St Andrews, Scotland KY16 9LZ\r<br>Office: UK+1334-461801  Admin: UK+1334-461842\r<br>\r<br>The University of St Andrews is a charity\r<br>registered in Scotland, No SC013532.\r<br>\r<br>"
    ]
   ]
